#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064006 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/id #uuid "15473603-5bb5-5a20-8fc9-e4d7589ccb38" :question/math-academy-id "e-473" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sin x}\\left(\\cot x + \\sin^2 x\\right)\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We multiply out the parentheses and then use the identity $\\dfrac{1}{\\sin x } = \\csc x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot {sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064007 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/id #uuid "68d2eb66-87ab-5182-9669-376bd1f70673" :question/math-academy-id "e-474" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos^2 x}\\,\\textrm d x =  \\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\dfrac{1}{\\cos x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe then use the fact that $\\dfrac{\\sin x }{\\cos x}  = \\tan x$ and $\\dfrac{1}{\\cos x} = \\sec x$ to express this as\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{\\pi/3} \\sec x\\tan x\\,\\textrm d x,\n$$\n\nwhich is a standard trigonometric integral. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064008 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/id #uuid "b34db891-0676-505c-9ba3-57d86f1d9b94" :question/math-academy-id "e-475" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the identity $\\tan 2x = \\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{\\cos 2x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\tan{2x}\\,\\textrm d x = \\int \\dfrac{\\sin{2x}}{\\cos 2x}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nNotice that the numerator of the integrand is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we can solve this by substitution.\n\nLet $u=\\cos 2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=-2 \\sin 2x \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad -\\dfrac 1 2 \\,\\textrm d u= \\sin 2x \\, \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)^{-1}|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sec}⁡2x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7542b0b-20cf-52a6-8889-23c81b40e0e5" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Quotient of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ff66d701-ccee-551c-96f2-ca7da2e776d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff66d701-ccee-551c-96f2-ca7da2e776d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298554} :answer-field/id #uuid "12018ba6-d93d-59f7-9f7e-13760e380cde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5555438-060c-5bd3-a19d-6d830dc51bce" :question/math-academy-id "q-352868" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We can rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡4x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}$ and $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡4x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡4x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=4x.$ Then $\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\text{d}u.$\n\nNow, we fill the table for the limits of integration, according to the rule $u=4x.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{12}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ |\n\nTherefore, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}(\\frac{1}{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d7bd68b3-56ae-57e5-81ef-2370c96664c4" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Product of Trigonometric Functions Using Trigonometric Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "844e4f20-3499-56a7-b3ba-041ac2f7e222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x - 2\\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063084} {:answer/id #uuid "a2a923ef-ba2c-556e-ba9b-9749fd371054" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan x - 2\\sin x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063085} {:answer/id #uuid "dc566e4e-6547-53f2-a82d-90720318a4bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\tan x + 2\\sin x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063086} {:answer/id #uuid "f25bc6ba-d12b-559f-9425-46b15ce536a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x + 2\\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "844e4f20-3499-56a7-b3ba-041ac2f7e222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sec x - 2\\cos x + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063084} :answer-field/id #uuid "452eae41-5b36-5f3b-b709-8daa01c4bad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063083}] :question/id #uuid "8beed7b3-f15a-500c-aa6e-e151d8a3ea87" :question/math-academy-id "q-49070" :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\tan x(\\sec x + 2\\cos x)dx$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a18e1647-4d67-547c-817b-4bf88fe7a925" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin (\\ln \\mid x \\mid) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063143} {:answer/id #uuid "a9bcfa03-a7b5-5f75-831a-32b948aa1346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid \\sin x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063144} {:answer/id #uuid "c61d5908-5c61-5619-be9c-4c5dbbea3949" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid \\csc x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063145} {:answer/id #uuid "dc21e438-0b01-592e-80b8-66a108ab490b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063146} {:answer/id #uuid "691ba9e1-89d0-515a-bea7-548f429be701" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\csc (\\ln \\mid x \\mid) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9bcfa03-a7b5-5f75-831a-32b948aa1346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln \\mid \\sin x \\mid + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063144} :answer-field/id #uuid "05533db7-22be-587e-a29e-1772ab898e00" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186063142}] :question/id #uuid "eeffd6d0-ba71-5af0-8a18-c001ad19ba1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-49259" :question/problem "What is $\\displaystyle \\int\\cot xdx$?"}]}
